From 119bf930dc7021455b693423d521876782a9b715 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: beardedeagle <randy@heroictek.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 20:51:55 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] feat(config): add a Metal tensor opt-out for GGUF models

Add defaults.disableMetalTensor to the global config. When true, the
llama.cpp backend sets GGML_METAL_TENSOR_DISABLE before loading the
runtime, so hosts whose Metal tensor kernels fail keep Metal GPU
offload without them. An explicit environment value still wins.
---
 docs/07-embedding.md                    | 13 +++++++++++
 src/engine/config.ts                    | 18 +++++++++++++-
 src/engine/models/backends/llama-cpp.ts | 15 ++++++++----
 test/config.test.mjs                    | 31 +++++++++++++++++++++++++
 4 files changed, 71 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/docs/07-embedding.md b/docs/07-embedding.md
index fa00f2b..e333247 100644
--- a/docs/07-embedding.md
+++ b/docs/07-embedding.md
@@ -113,6 +113,19 @@ The equivalent environment override is `ZVEC_GREP_DEVICE`. Model2Vec models
 such as Potion use static vector lookup, so selecting a GPU does not improve
 their runtime.
 
+On macOS, GGUF models use Metal tensor kernels when the GPU supports them. If
+those kernels fail on a host, skip them while keeping Metal GPU offload by
+setting `defaults.disableMetalTensor` in `~/.zvec-grep/config.json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "version": 1,
+  "defaults": { "disableMetalTensor": true }
+}
+```
+
+The setting applies to every GGUF model loaded by the process.
+
 Override the download cache with `--model-cache` or `ZVEC_GREP_MODEL_CACHE`:
 
 ```bash
